<header><p class="enc-kicker">Person</p><h1 class="enc-title">Toussaint Louverture</h1></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">Toussaint Louverture was the leading figure of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/haitian-revolution">Haitian Revolution</a>, a formerly enslaved man who became the commander of the revolutionary forces in the French colony of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/saint-domingue">Saint-Domingue</a>. He defeated rival forces and governed the colony before being captured by <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/napoleon">Napoleon</a>'s expedition and dying in a French prison in 1803. Accounts of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/french-colonial-empire">French Empire</a> discuss the revolution, which produced independent <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/haiti">Haiti</a> in 1804, as a major blow to French colonial power.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/toussaint-louverture.jpg" alt="Toussaint Louverture" width="224" height="398" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: Pierre-Charles Baquoy (1759-1829) · Public domain · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Toussaint_Louverture_par_Pierre-Charles_Baquoy.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
